meta {
  name: Add a game
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}{{apiPrefix}}/me/games
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "Hollow Knight",
    "system": "Nintendo Switch",
    "genres": ["Metroidvania"],
    "releaseDate": "2017-02-24",
    "imageUrl": "",
    "igdbId": null,
    "rating": ""
  }
}

docs {
  Adds a game to the caller's library. Only `name` and `system` are required;
  every IGDB-derived field is optional so a hand-entered title works.

  `igdbId` decides catalog identity. With one, the shared `game_metadata` row is
  looked up or created and everyone who adds that game lands on it. Without one,
  the row is private to its creator, because a hand-typed name is not a canonical
  key and one person's typo must not rewrite someone else's shelf.

  `imageUrl` accepts only `https://images.igdb.com/` URLs or `""`. Covers are
  hotlinked, so an open URL field would make any library free image hosting.

  Genres sent here are only a FALLBACK. When the add creates a new catalog row the
  server looks the genres up from Wikipedia, because IGDB's are too coarse for a
  library. Ask `Catalog and search / Preview a catalog entry` what it will actually
  store.

  Returns the created game in the same wire shape as the public read.

  - 201 created
  - 403 not onboarded, or library at MAX_GAMES
  - 409 that game is already in the library
  - 422 blank name or system, unknown rating, non-IGDB imageUrl
  - 429 over the shared 60-writes-per-minute budget
  - 503 writes disabled (preview deploys hold a read-only Postgres role)
}
